10891	RISK-285	A0939_Gipiškis2024	Incorporating the estimation of atypical input samples or classes for better model reliability	Model Safety Engineering	Incorporating the estimation of rare atypical input samples or classes might improve a model’s reliability, both with respect to its predictions and confidence calibration. Model predictions for rare inputs and classes may have a tendency of being overconfident and have worse accuracy scores [232]. For LLMs, the negative log-likelihood can be used as an atypicality measure. For discriminative models, Gaussian Mixture Models can be employed to estimate conditional and marginal distributions, which are then used in atypicality measurement.
